1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,159
When everyone loves the same pet, care can still get lost between family members.

2
00:00:05,359 --> 00:00:10,377
Paw Twin gives Emma and Alex a shared daily routine for their fictional cat, Mochi.

3
00:00:12,883 --> 00:00:17,292
Emma opens the household application to see Mochi's records and today's care.

4
00:00:17,492 --> 00:00:23,056
Alex has a separate account, so family actions can be attributed to the person who records them.

5
00:00:25,617 --> 00:00:31,595
Two sample weights, seven days apart, produce a report showing a hundred fifty gram increase.

6
00:00:31,795 --> 00:00:37,688
The calculation links to its records, while actual food intake stays unknown until someone records it.

7
00:00:40,217 --> 00:00:44,100
Emma records that Mochi likes a feather toy and rests after meals.

8
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:49,314
She confirms this preference before it becomes part of the shared context for future answers.

9
00:00:51,817 --> 00:00:56,800
Alex opens a new conversation in the Alexa web simulator and asks what Mochi likes.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,004
The reply reads the confirmed preference from the shared record and exposes the source for inspection.

11
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:08,779
Emma asks the simulator to plan playtime and reviews the proposed care.

12
00:01:08,979 --> 00:01:13,702
She chooses a time, keeping the request as a draft while she checks the details.

13
00:01:16,217 --> 00:01:19,591
The prepared proposal shows what will be arranged for Mochi.

14
00:01:19,791 --> 00:01:25,514
At this stage, no care task has been created; Emma still controls the final confirmation.

15
00:01:28,017 --> 00:01:32,410
Emma confirms the arrangement, creating one shared manual care task.

16
00:01:32,610 --> 00:01:37,864
The result says it is waiting for a person, keeping a planned action distinct from a completed one.

17
00:01:40,417 --> 00:01:44,940
Alex claims the task and records the playtime as completed through his own account.

18
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:51,378
The household history identifies who recorded the result, helping the next family member understand the handoff.

19
00:01:53,883 --> 00:01:57,837
Emma's daily report now counts one completed manual care task.

20
00:01:58,037 --> 00:02:03,496
The report summarizes recorded care without inventing food intake or making a clinical diagnosis.

21
00:02:06,017 --> 00:02:11,840
When someone else helps, Emma can issue time-limited, read-only access for one selected pet.

22
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:17,968
The caregiver can inspect the shared context, while the owner keeps control over changes and revocation.

23
00:02:20,483 --> 00:02:25,322
Before a vet visit, Emma creates a dated information card from Mochi's records.

24
00:02:25,522 --> 00:02:30,925
It separates known facts from missing information and can be printed or saved as an offline document.

25
00:02:33,483 --> 00:02:39,342
Paw Twin connects records, confirmed care, family handoffs, and reports in one inspectable routine.

26
00:02:39,542 --> 00:02:47,714
This demonstration runs locally with rules and SQLite; Alexa and devices are simulated, and live cloud integration remains unverified.
